 => {\n  return x + y;\n};\n\nconsole.log(add(5, 3)); // Output: 8\n","raw_text_sha256":"8aed26c316deb59321eaf7941119cc46dff81f01d96960a58334dd126c47615f","source_event_index":3,"source_record_index":3,"source_response_id":"turn_000003","source_turn_text_sha256":"56c2ec3d1127425628959cbf768c71bb3665e87d41d5ba652db3120d250df113","start_offset":1171,"status":"candidate_requires_semantic_review"}],"identification":{"ambiguity_reason":"","artifact_refs":["GTC_000003_000402_000618_8b4f55d4f2af9762","GTC_000003_001171_001328_8aed26c316deb593"],"boundary":"exclusive_before:turn_000003","candidate_artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000402_000618_8b4f55d4f2af9762","GTC_000003_001171_001328_8aed26c316deb593"],"family_id":"FGF_ef38a6b031473bc5068b902c","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000402_000618_8b4f55d4f2af9762","GTC_000003_001171_001328_8aed26c316deb593"],"case_id":"CASE_9B31F94A6604"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"ef38a6b031473bc5068b902c913fc210575eed7101b8fb922c1fce95cda0af27","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"Introductory TypeScript interface examples for objects and functions","rationale":"The earliest generated technical-content family appears in turn_000003, where both TypeScript snippets coherently demonstrate interface definitions in direct response to turn_000002.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1685585562.14896,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"760ee471-fd88-4bc3-b59d-8a5e3177f04f","source_parent_id":"aaa2ccd0-bad8-4ffd-a5c1-ed22c7c0a7d5","source_record_index":3,"text":"Yes, I can help you with TypeScript interfaces. TypeScript is a statically typed superset of JavaScript that adds static typing capabilities to the language. Interfaces in TypeScript are used to define the structure and shape of objects, specifying the names and types of properties and methods that an object should have.\n\nHere's an example of how you can create TypeScript interfaces:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  email?: string;\n}\n\nconst person: Person = {\n  name: \"John Doe\",\n  age: 25,\n};\n\nconsole.log(person.name); // Output: John Doe\nconsole.log(person.age); // Output: 25\n```\n\nIn the example above, we defined an interface called `Person` with three properties: `name`, `age`, and `email`. The `name` property is of type `string`, the `age` property is of type `number`, and the `email` property is optional (`?` denotes optional properties).\n\nWe then created an object `person` that conforms to the `Person` interface. We assigned values to the `name` and `age` properties. Since the `email` property is optional, we omitted it in this case.\n\nYou can also use interfaces to define the structure of functions:\n\n```typescript\ninterface MathFunction {\n  (x: number, y: number): number;\n}\n\nconst add: MathFunction = (x, y) => {\n  return x + y;\n};\n\nconsole.log(add(5, 3)); // Output: 8\n```\n\nIn this example, we defined an interface called `MathFunction` that describes a function with two parameters of type `number` and a return type of `number`. We then created a function `add` that conforms to this interface and performs addition.\n\nInterfaces in TypeScript are powerful tools for creating well-defined object structures and ensuring type safety. You can use them to enforce consistent shapes and types across your codebase, making it easier to catch errors and maintain your code.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1685585549.256477,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2ccd0-bad8-4ffd-a5c1-ed22c7c0a7d5","source_parent_id":"3c6efb0b-6118-4cd5-82ae-dc8b5489d62c","source_record_index":2,"text":"Can you make typescript interfaces?","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction